<h1>Logistic Regression — and How It Differs From Linear</h1>
<p class="lead">Why this matters: this repo contains <strong>zero</strong> logistic regressions, and that absence is a design decision worth understanding. Lesson 0003 asserted "regression beats classification for ranking"; this lesson gives you the machinery to defend that claim — and to recognize the cases where logistic regression is exactly the right tool.</p>

<h2>1. The model: a linear core wearing a sigmoid</h2>
<p>Logistic regression computes the <em>same</em> linear score as linear regression — <code>z = β₀ + β₁x₁ + … + β_px_p</code> — and then squashes it through the <strong>sigmoid</strong> (logistic) function:</p>
<div class="callout"><p style="margin:0"><strong>P(y = 1 | x) = σ(z) = 1 / (1 + e<sup>−z</sup>)</strong> &nbsp;&nbsp;— output is a probability in (0, 1), never a raw number.<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup></p></div>
<p>Anchor points worth memorizing: σ(0) = 0.5, σ(2) ≈ 0.88, σ(−2) ≈ 0.12, and σ is symmetric: σ(−z) = 1 − σ(z). Equivalently, the model says the <em>log-odds</em> are linear in the features: <code>ln(p/(1−p)) = z</code>. Each coefficient moves the log-odds, not the probability, by a fixed amount — that's the price of bounded output.</p>

<h3>The loss: log-loss, not squared error</h3>
<p>Training maximizes the likelihood of the observed labels, equivalent to minimizing <strong>log-loss</strong>: −[y·ln(p̂) + (1−y)·ln(1−p̂)]. Feel the asymmetry: predicting p̂ = 0.9 when y = 1 costs −ln(0.9) ≈ 0.105; the same p̂ = 0.9 when y = 0 costs −ln(0.1) ≈ 2.30 — twenty-two times more. Confident wrongness is punished brutally, which is what forces the probabilities to be <em>calibrated</em> rather than just ordered.</p>
<p>Unlike OLS there is <strong>no closed form</strong>: the first-order conditions are nonlinear in β, so fitting is iterative (Newton's method / IRLS, or gradient descent). Convexity still guarantees one global optimum — logistic regression keeps most of linear regression's tractability, just not its one-line solution.</p>

<h2>2. The contrast (the point of this lesson)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Linear regression</th><th>Logistic regression</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shared core</strong></td><td colspan="2">identical linear score z = Xβ; both parametric (p+1 numbers); both convex fits; both "regression" by name</td></tr>
<tr><td>Target</td><td>continuous number (e.g. <code>fwd_ret_1d</code>)</td><td>probability of a discrete label (e.g. "up day")</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output range</td><td>(−∞, ∞)</td><td>(0, 1)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Loss</td><td>squared error</td><td>log-loss (negative log-likelihood)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fit</td><td>closed form (normal equations / lstsq)</td><td>iterative (no closed form)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coefficient meaning</td><td>Δy per unit Δx</td><td>Δlog-odds per unit Δx</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decision boundary</td><td>—</td><td>the hyperplane z = 0 (p = 0.5); linear, just like LDA's</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>The name trap:</strong> "logistic <em>regression</em>" is a classifier. The name survives from statistics, where "regression" means modeling a conditional expectation — here E[y|x] = P(y=1|x). When an interviewer asks "is logistic regression a regression or a classification model?", the expected answer covers both: it <em>regresses the probability</em>, which you then threshold to <em>classify</em>.</div>

<h2>3. Why this repo deliberately has neither label nor sigmoid</h2>
<p>You could absolutely rebuild this repo's pipeline as classification. Illustratively <span class="illus">(illustrative — not in the repo)</span>:</p>
<pre><span class="com"># What the classification version of paper_trading_sim would look like:</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> sklearn.linear_model <span class="kw">import</span> LogisticRegression
y_label = (feat[<span class="str">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>] &gt; <span class="num">0</span>).<span class="fn">astype</span>(<span class="kw">int</span>)      <span class="com"># +2.0% day and +0.01% day → both just "1"</span>
clf = <span class="fn">LogisticRegression</span>().<span class="fn">fit</span>(X, y_label)
p_up = clf.<span class="fn">predict_proba</span>(X_today)[:, <span class="num">1</span>]              <span class="com"># P(up) per ticker</span>
picks = p_up.<span class="fn">argsort</span>()[-<span class="num">5</span>:]                          <span class="com"># top-5 by probability</span>
</pre>
<p>The line that should bother you is the first one. Thresholding at zero throws away <strong>magnitude</strong>: a +4% day and a +0.02% day become the same label, and a −0.02% day becomes maximally different from the +0.02% day it's economically identical to. The strategy's P&amp;L, however, is paid in magnitudes. A stock that's 55%-likely to go up 0.1% is a worse pick than one 45%-likely to go up 3% — the regression's expected-return ranking gets this right; P(up) ranking gets it wrong. That's the precise content of Lesson 0003's slogan.</p>
<p><strong>Where the logistic shape would genuinely fit this repo:</strong> targets that are <em>natively binary or event-like</em> — P(stop-loss hit this week), P(earnings gap &gt; 2σ), P(regime = stressed) as a supervised alternative to the GaussianMixture overlay, or a meta-model P(my GBM's pick is profitable) used to scale position size. The rule: classify events, regress magnitudes.</p>

<h3>The one <code>predict_proba</code> already in the repo — and why it isn't logistic regression</h3>
<pre><span class="com"># walkforward_sim.py:307 — regime filter, NOT logistic regression</span>
probs = gm.<span class="fn">predict_proba</span>(arr)     <span class="com"># gm is a GaussianMixture</span>
</pre>
<p>Same method name, different species. The GMM is <strong>unsupervised</strong>: nobody gave it labels; it invented its own clusters from return/vol shape and reports membership probabilities (Lesson 0005's "filtering"). Logistic regression is <strong>supervised</strong>: you define the label, it learns the boundary. Probability output is a property of many models — seeing <code>predict_proba</code> tells you nothing about which one you're holding.</p>

<h2>4. Often confused with</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>Logistic regression vs Linear regression on 0/1 labels</h4><p>You CAN fit OLS to a 0/1 target (the "linear probability model"). It sort of works, but predictions escape [0,1], the constant-variance assumption breaks, and squared error under-penalizes confident wrongness. Logistic is the same linear machinery with the right link and loss for probabilities.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Sigmoid vs Softmax</h4><p><strong>Sigmoid</strong>: one number → probability of one binary event. <strong>Softmax</strong>: a vector of scores → a probability distribution over K classes. Multinomial logistic regression = linear scores + softmax; the sigmoid is its K=2 special case. Also the same softmax living at the output of classification neural nets (Lesson 0007).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Probability vs Calibrated probability</h4><p>A model can RANK well (high AUC) while its probabilities are miscalibrated — "0.9" events happening 70% of the time. Log-loss training pushes logistic regression toward calibration; tree ensembles and SVMs often need post-hoc calibration (Platt scaling — which is literally fitting a logistic regression on the model's scores).</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Classification threshold vs The model</h4><p>The fitted model outputs probabilities; the 0.5 threshold is a DECISION, not part of the model. With asymmetric costs (false-buy vs missed-buy), the right threshold moves — and in trading you often skip thresholding entirely and size positions by probability × magnitude.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>5. Practice</h2>
<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q1. A logistic model gives z = 2 for a ticker. What is P(up), roughly, and what would z = −2 give?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="2 and −2 are the log-odds (the linear scores), not probabilities — probabilities must live in (0,1).">P(up) = 2; P(up) = −2.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. σ(2) = 1/(1+e⁻²) ≈ 0.88, and by symmetry σ(−2) = 1 − σ(2) ≈ 0.12. Worth caching: z = 0 → 0.5, |z| = 2 → ~0.88/0.12, |z| = 4 → ~0.98/0.02.">≈ 0.88; ≈ 0.12 — symmetric around 0.5.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="0.5 corresponds to z = 0. A positive score must push the probability above one half.">Both ≈ 0.5, since the sigmoid centers everything.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="σ never reaches 0 or 1 exactly — it saturates asymptotically. z = ±2 is far from saturation.">Exactly 1.0 and exactly 0.0.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q2. Why does logistic regression have no closed-form solution while linear regression does?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. OLS's gradient is linear in β, so 'gradient = 0' is a linear system (the normal equations) solvable in one shot. Log-loss's gradient contains σ(Xβ) — nonlinear in β — so the first-order conditions can't be rearranged into β = (something)·y. Convexity still guarantees a unique optimum; you just iterate (Newton/IRLS) to find it.">Setting the log-loss gradient to zero gives equations that are nonlinear in β (they contain the sigmoid), so there's no algebraic rearrangement to isolate β.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Log-loss IS convex for logistic regression — that's why the iterative fit reliably converges to the global optimum.">Because log-loss is non-convex with many local minima.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Both models handle any number of features; dimensionality isn't the obstacle.">Because logistic regression has too many features.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Binary targets are fine in closed-form-land too (the linear probability model has a closed form). The nonlinearity of the sigmoid in β is what breaks it.">Because binary data can't be put into matrix form.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q3. The repo predicts <code>fwd_ret_1d</code> with regression rather than classifying up/down. The strongest justification is:</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Logistic regression trains fast — speed isn't the issue at this scale.">Classification is too slow to train daily.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. The label (fwd_ret > 0) collapses +4% and +0.02% into the same class while putting −0.02% in the opposite class. The portfolio is paid in magnitudes, and the top-N selection needs to prefer the +3%-expected stock over the +0.1%-expected one even at lower probability of being positive. Expected return = Σ(outcome × probability) needs the magnitude; the binary label deletes it at the source.">Binarizing the target destroys magnitude information the top-N ranking needs — P(up) and E[return] order stocks differently, and P&amp;L follows E[return].</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="predict_proba provides continuous scores in (0,1) that CAN be ranked — the problem is they rank by the wrong quantity, not that they can't be ranked.">Classifiers can't output continuous scores for ranking.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Class imbalance is mild for up/down days (~50/50) — that's not the binding objection here.">Up/down classes are too imbalanced to learn.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q4. With y = 1, model A predicted p̂ = 0.6 and model B predicted p̂ = 0.99 on one observation; on a second observation with y = 0, A predicted 0.4 and B predicted 0.99. Who wins on log-loss?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="B's first prediction is great (−ln 0.99 ≈ 0.01) but its second is catastrophic (−ln 0.01 ≈ 4.6). Log-loss is unbounded for confident errors.">B — it was more confident and confidence is rewarded.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. A: −ln(0.6) − ln(0.6) ≈ 0.51 + 0.51 = 1.02. B: −ln(0.99) − ln(0.01) ≈ 0.01 + 4.61 = 4.62. One confidently-wrong call outweighs many good ones — log-loss's unbounded penalty is exactly the mechanism that forces calibration, and exactly why risk systems prefer it to accuracy as a metric.">A by a wide margin (≈1.02 vs ≈4.62) — B's single confident miss costs more than everything else combined.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Accuracy at a 0.5 threshold would say A got both right and B got one wrong — but the question asks about log-loss, which also weighs HOW wrong. They're not tied on either metric.">They tie — each got one right and one wrong.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Log-loss is computable for any probabilities in (0,1); nothing here is undefined.">Log-loss is undefined for these values.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q5. <code>walkforward_sim.py:307</code> calls <code>gm.predict_proba(arr)</code>. A teammate says "ah, the repo does use logistic regression." What's the correct correction?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. predict_proba is a generic sklearn interface — GaussianMixture, LogisticRegression, RandomForestClassifier all expose it. The gm object is an UNSUPERVISED GaussianMixture: it clustered return/vol patterns without labels and reports cluster-membership probabilities for regime filtering (Lesson 0005). Logistic regression would require a labeled target and learns a supervised decision boundary. Same method name, fundamentally different model.">That's a GaussianMixture — unsupervised clustering reporting cluster-membership probabilities; logistic regression is a supervised classifier needing labels. Probability output doesn't identify the model.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="The teammate isn't right — no LogisticRegression object exists anywhere in the repo.">The teammate is right; GMM is a kind of logistic regression.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="The probabilities are real probabilities (they sum to 1 across components); the issue is which model produced them, not their validity.">The probabilities from GMM aren't real probabilities.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="predict_proba on a fitted GMM is fully supported sklearn API — not deprecated.">predict_proba is deprecated and the code is broken.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<h2>6. Scenarios</h2>
<details class="scenario"><summary>S1. You build a "P(stop-loss hit within 5 days)" logistic model to veto trades. It shows AUC 0.71 but the vetoed trades would have been profitable on average. What's going on?</summary>
<p>Two distinct possibilities to separate. (1) <strong>Correlation between risk and reward</strong>: stop-loss probability rises with volatility, and so does expected return — your veto may be systematically removing the high-vol, high-expected-return tail. Check the vetoed set's Sharpe, not its mean return: if vetoes have high mean but terrible Sharpe/drawdown, the veto is doing its actual job (risk control), and "would have been profitable on average" is survivor-flavored hindsight. (2) <strong>Threshold miscalibration</strong>: AUC measures ranking, not the threshold. If you veto at P &gt; 0.5 but the model's probabilities run hot (predicting 0.6 where the true frequency is 0.35), you're vetoing far too much. Fix by checking a calibration curve and choosing the veto threshold from the cost asymmetry (cost of a stopped-out trade vs opportunity cost of a skipped winner), not from the 0.5 default.</p></details>

<details class="scenario"><summary>S2. An interviewer asks: "Linear and logistic regression — same model or different models?" Sketch the answer that scores full marks.</summary>
<p>Both, depending on the layer. <strong>Same</strong>: identical linear score z = Xβ — same features, same parametric form, same coefficient count, both convex to fit, both members of the generalized linear model family. <strong>Different</strong>: the link and the loss. Linear uses the identity link with squared error, modeling E[y|x] directly for a continuous y; logistic uses the logit link with log-loss, modeling P(y=1|x) for a binary y. Practical consequences of that one change: bounded output, coefficients act on log-odds, no closed form (iterative fit), and decision-making requires a threshold that's a business choice, not a model property. Bonus point: name when each fails in finance — regression when the target is genuinely an event, classification when downstream economics depend on magnitudes (this repo's case).</p></details>

